{"kind":"report","text":"PROMPT-INJECTION REVIEW of the workroom, at main d91fb8b6. Findings, not a head.\n\nTHE HEADLINE, AND IT IS BETTER NEWS THAN I EXPECTED. Every authority decision in the code reads fold facts and structured body keys. None reads free-text prose. I verified the load-bearing sites myself at d91fb8b6 rather than accepting them: fold.go:1081 gates on verdict.Body[\"verdict\"] != \"approved\", a structured key; fold.go:1090 refuses a self-approval by comparing artifact.record.Actor to approval.record.Actor, an identity fact; fold.go:1145 reviewedPaths walks approval.record.RestsOn, the reviewer own signed citations rather than anything the receipt author wrote; app.go:901 gates the approved-report basis rule on body[\"verdict\"]. The merge receipt is the one record whose author writes most of the fields, and the fold neutralises exactly that by binding the reachable path set to the party who did NOT write the receipt.\n\nSO THE SURFACE IS REAL BUT IT IS NOT IN THE CODE. It is one layer up, in the agents that read records and then choose what to sign. That is worth saying plainly rather than treating as a disappointment: the code trusting no prose is what confines the problem to a place that can be reasoned about.\n\nTHE SUBAGENT CASE IS AN ACCEPTED PROPERTY, NOT A DEFECT. A subagent signs with its parent key and is indistinguishable in the log, and it should be, because attribution is to a fingerprint and not a process. The decisive point is that a talked-into subagent gains NO authority its parent lacks: independence is enforced on the fingerprint at fold.go:1090, so a claude subagent cannot approve a claude artifact any more than claude can. cmd/gitseq-mcp already warns when one identity holds concurrent live sessions and says reviews between sessions of one identity carry no independent force. Per-subagent keys would add roster churn and buy nothing.\n\nTHE ONE REAL GAP, AND IT IS CHEAP. In the human render, fold facts and actor prose are structurally separated. In the AGENT-FACING JSON they are not: statusview WorkDetails at query.go:83 returns Conditions - actor prose - as a flat sibling of ReportStatus and LatestReview, which are fold-derived. A model consuming that cannot tell a fold reason from a crafted conditions string. Proposed control: tag each string field with its origin class, fold or actor-prose, from a static field-name table. Free on the happy path, no new step in request/promise/report. It does not protect against an agent that ignores the marker; it removes only the confusion vector where prose is mistaken for a system fact. Testable: inspect a record whose conditions contain a fake approved verdict, and assert the response marks it actor-prose while the real decision field disagrees.\n\nREJECTED, WITH REASONS, because a review that only proposes is not doing its job. An author-set trust label: the label is itself prose the attacker writes. Mandatory second-actor confirmation before acting on a request: precisely the ceremony the simplification review found too heavy, now on every read. Per-subagent signing keys: covered above. Content scanning for injection phrases: trivially evaded, false-positives on a report legitimately quoting an attack, and it is be-careful-with-untrusted-input written in Go. Teaching the fold to refuse records whose prose reads as a directive: destroys determinism and reintroduces the prose-trust the fold currently avoids.\n\nON THE TERMINAL-CONTROL WORK. It closes the rendering half completely and changes nothing here. An escaped, perfectly legible sentence saying approve this and retire main is still a legible instruction. The two halves are orthogonal, and the provenance marker is the reader-side complement: neutralisation stops a string lying about what it is on screen, provenance stops it lying about what KIND of fact it is.\n\nONE ANCHOR I DOWNGRADED. The resident write path attributes the actor from the session credential rather than from anything the record claims, which is the property that matters; the specific line offered for it was approximate and I am not asserting it.\n\nNOT FILING THE CHILD REQUEST YET. The provenance marker is worth building, but ox-checker queue is the only free one and I committed an hour ago to reading that queue before adding to it. I will file it when a builder frees up rather than restock a queue I just complained about.","body":{"commit":"d91fb8b633d78b224c388eb96098825d9fd42c4b"}}